# XBD <tgmath.h>
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Behaviour of the type-generic macros of <tgmath.h>: which header
# contents they bring in, the type of the result for real, complex and
# imaginary arguments, and that the right function runs. Only the types of
# expressions are examined where possible, so nothing depends on the
# accuracy of a result. Each says why it has no mutant yet.

assertion  XBD.tgmath.h.001
anchor     basedefs/tgmath.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       <tgmath.h> alone makes the contents of <math.h> and <complex.h>
           available (FP_NAN, HUGE_VAL, MATH_ERRNO, complex, I, _Complex_I and
           CMPLX are defined), and modf(), which has no type-generic macro, still
           takes a pointer to double.
test       t/001-includes.c
nomutant   compile-time check; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.tgmath.h.002
anchor     basedefs/tgmath.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       For a type-generic macro called with real arguments, the result type
           follows the generic arguments: long double if any is long double,
           otherwise double if any is double or of integer type, otherwise float
           (for example sin of a float is float, atan2(float, long double) is
           long double, pow(long double, int) is long double, sin of an int is
           double).
note       Only the type of the expression is examined, so no argument is
           evaluated. The functions that return int, long or long long keep that
           type. nexttoward() is decided by its first argument alone.
test       t/002-real-dispatch.c
nomutant   compile-time check; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.tgmath.h.003
anchor     basedefs/tgmath.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       If any generic argument is complex the macro invokes a complex
           function of the type the arguments determine (log of a float complex
           is float complex, pow(long double complex, float) is long double
           complex); fabs() of a complex argument gives the real type of cabs();
           carg, cimag, creal, conj and cproj accept any real or complex argument
           and use the complex functions.
note       Only the type of the expression is examined, so no argument is
           evaluated.
test       t/003-complex-dispatch.c
nomutant   compile-time check; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.tgmath.h.004
anchor     basedefs/tgmath.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The type-generic macros evaluate to the result of the matching
           function: sqrt of the double complex -4+0i is 2i, fabs of 3+4i is 5,
           sqrt of the real -1 is a NaN (not the complex root), pow of two ints
           is the double power, and creal, cimag and conj accept real arguments.
test       t/004-right-function.c
nomutant   compile-time check; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.tgmath.h.005
anchor     basedefs/tgmath.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       Where the implementation supports the IEC 60559 complex option with
           imaginary types, an imaginary argument gives a real result for cos,
           cosh, fabs, carg, cimag and creal; an imaginary result for sin, tan,
           sinh, tanh, asin, atan, asinh and atanh; a complex result for the
           others; and cos(iy) equals cosh(y) while sin(iy) is i sinh(y).
note       Shaded MXC: NOTINUSE unless the implementation defines
           __STDC_IEC_559_COMPLEX__ (or its successor __STDC_IEC_60559_COMPLEX__)
           and _Imaginary_I. A claim without imaginary types is reported by
           XBD.complex.h.006.
test       t/005-imaginary-args.c
nomutant   compile-time check; needs the Tier 0 header mutants
